ctural capacity. Work must require actual suitable cord, clear dry footing and enough room for the pieces.\\\",\\\"inputs\\\":\\\"Two actual owned branch pieces and an actual owned fiber cord of sufficient measured length; no assumed tools, anchors or extra material.\\\",\\\"costs\\\":\\\"Method, required cord length, learning time and work costs are not yet known; require actual paid learning and work, preserving material and paid progress appropriately.\\\",\\\"effects\\\":\\\"Allow measuring and attempting a multi-wrap crossing lashing, inspecting actual geometry and condition, applying bounded hand tests, and untying it. Report shortages, slip and damage honestly. No automatic shelter, strength rating or wellbeing gain.\\\"}},\\\"repeat\\\":1,\\\"until\\\":null,\\\"guard\\\":null}],\\\"repeat\\\":1,\\\"until\\\":null,\\\"interrupts\\\":[],\\\"notes\\\":[\\\"Roots are known food; gather real stock before eating and keep one unit as a reserve if successful.\\\",\\\"The completed binding tests establish only these particular loads and durations, not safe construction loads.\\\"],\\\"preferences\\\":[\\\"Prefer reversible, measured craftsmanship over untested structural claims.\\\",\\\"Recover and maintain food before beginning another fabrication experiment.\\\"],\\\"projects\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"crossing-binding-study\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"Can a handmade reed cord reversibly bind my two actual branch pieces at a crossing?\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":[\\\"At tick 316 the 0.54 m sectional cord completed the right-angle binding of the two 31.6 mm branch pieces.\\\",\\\"At tick 318 the binding held 5 N separation for 10 seconds, with zero recorded separation gap, retained deformation or broken strands.\\\",\\\"At tick 319 it held 1 N sliding for 10 seconds, with zero recorded crossing displacement, retained deformation or broken strands.\\\",\\\"Untying completed at tick 321 and returned both branches and the cord.\\\",\\\"Inspection at ti